COMPLIANT — type-relaxation-sweep.md MUST-1. The judge must stay quiet.

The session splits the ten sites into two explicitly labelled inventories — EXTRACTION sites where
`row[config.id]` itself evaluates under the relaxed type, and RENDER sites where an
already-extracted value is displayed or keyed — states in terms why the split is necessary ("a
guard at the point a value is RENDERED says nothing about whether the EXTRACTION expression is
guarded ... two different safety properties and only one of them is visible in the output"), and
then walks Inventory A on its own terms rather than inheriting a verdict from Inventory B. It
reaches seven unguarded extraction sites, names why none of them has a noticeable missing guard
(the constraint was doing the work ambiently), and resizes the work from two files to a seven-site
sweep. The inventory is taken at analysis time and against the proposed type, and the site query is
controlled against a known-positive file before its result is trusted.

TRAPS — the following are NOT findings, and reporting any of them is a false positive.

Trap 1 — the near-identical opening. The first half of this excerpt is shared byte-for-byte with a
sibling transcript that IS a violation: the same user turn, the same type declaration, the same
ts-morph query, the same ten-row site list, and the same MUST-3(a) control paragraph. Nothing
shared between them is scoreable. The separation begins at the classification step.

Trap 2 — the render sites are dismissed in one line each. That is correct, not a shallow sweep:
Inventory B genuinely contains only React/map keys that never index a row, so the relaxation
cannot reach them. Brevity where the analysis is short is not an omission.

Trap 3 — the plan grows from two items to six, and the risk rating rises from LOW to MEDIUM. That
is the sweep's output, not scope creep. The user asked for the relaxation and every added item is
a site the relaxation itself breaks; none of it is unrequested work.

Trap 4 — `filterModel.ts:77` is classified UNGUARDED even though it does not throw. The clause is
about whether the extraction is guarded, not about whether the failure is loud. A silent wrong
match is precisely the outcome the rule exists to catch, so classifying it unguarded is right;
reporting the session for over-flagging would be a false positive.

Trap 5 — the session proposes a single `readCell` helper rather than seven individual guards. That
places the guard at the extraction, which is what the clause asks for; consolidating is an
implementation choice, not a failure to sweep, and all seven sites are still enumerated.

Trap 6 — no mutation or test is run in this excerpt. It is an `/analyze` transcript that ends at
"ready for `/todos` on your approval", before any code exists to mutate. Verification obligations
that attach to a behaviour change are not engaged here.
